faq:
  - q: "Why private pay rather than insurance?"
    a: "<p>Practically: it means no diagnosis requirement, no cap on the number of sessions, no waiting on authorizations, and no pressure to shape your child's goals to fit a billing category. It also means I can work in your home and out in the community, which many plans don't cover well.</p><p>The trade-off is real and worth saying plainly: the cost sits with your family rather than an insurer. For some families that's the reason this works, and for some it's the reason it doesn't. Either is a fine answer, and the intro call is free precisely so you can find out which before spending anything.</p>"
  - q: "Would I get a superbill to claim back?"
    a: "<p><span class=\"todo-inline\">[Unresolved — see TODO.md. This must be answered before launch. If superbills are offered, say so plainly here and explain what a family does with one. If not, say that too, so nobody is surprised.]</span></p>"
  - q: "How many sessions will we need?"
    a: "There's no set answer, and I'd be suspicious of anyone who gave you one before meeting your child. Some families come weekly for a stretch and stop. Others check in every few weeks, or come back when something changes. We decide the rhythm together after the first session and revisit it whenever it stops fitting."
  - q: "Can I stop, or take a break?"
    a: "Any time, and without explaining yourself. There's no package to buy and nothing to cancel out of. Families pause for holidays, for illness, for money, or because things are going well — all normal."
  - q: "What's your cancellation policy?"
    a: "<span class=\"todo-inline\">[cancellation policy]</span>"
  - q: "Do you take on families outside East Jefferson County?"
    a: "The practice is built around in-home and community sessions, so the service area is genuinely a limit rather than a preference. If you're just outside it, ask — it's often workable. Parent and family consulting is sometimes possible at more distance."
